+                     * NOP. ml962 logs the slot after the fact and shows it is not
+                     * always a NOP; when it is live code the barrier permanently
+                     * destroys an instruction in a block that runs again. If that
+                     * instruction built the high half of a pointer, the pointer is
+                     * truncated to 32 bits and a later dereference faults. Crash
+                     * Bandicoot N. Sane Trilogy (iDeck 1.4.6 build 33) dies exactly
+                     * like that: ml962 "OPERAND IS FULL WIDTH -> the LOAD lost the
+                     * high half", then an AV at [x20+0x30] with x20=0 in ntdll.
+                     *
+                     * When the neighbour is not a NOP, leave the JIT code untouched
+                     * and emulate this one access through mach_vm, the way the
+                     * misaligned CAS/LSE paths below do. Full fences around it keep
+                     * the acquire/release ordering of the original instruction. The
+                     * next unaligned execution faults and is emulated again: slower,
+                     * but correct. A NOP neighbour keeps the existing fast rewrite. */
+                    int ideck_emulated = 0;
+                    {
+                        int is_load  = (insn & LDAXR_MASK) == LDAR_INST ||
